 "style": "ps2",
 "title": "THE MILGRAM EXPERIMENT",
 "angle": "Would you hurt someone just because an authority told you to?",
 "answer": "In Milgram's best-known version 65% went to 450 volts, but obedience collapsed when the setting lost prestige, the authority was remote or peers refused, and when told outright they had no choice, people stopped: they were serving a cause they believed in rather than obeying orders, and many doubted the shocks were real.",
 "logline": "Two in three pressed the 450-volt switch, so would you hurt someone on command? Milgram's 1961 Yale study used an actor and fake shocks, asking the Eichmann question, but obedience wasn't fixed: a shabby office, a phone, rebel peers all cut it, but the strongest clue was the words, because people stopped when told they had no choice, so they were serving science, not orders, but Milgram's files show many doubted the shocks, so it shows how far we go for something we trust.",
 "hook_text": "THE MAN NEXT DOOR WENT QUIET",
 "hooks": [
  "When told 'you have no other choice', Milgram's volunteers stopped.",
  "In a shabby office instead of Yale, obedience dropped to 48 percent."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "room"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "Two out of three people pressed the switch marked 450 volts.",
   "say": "Two out of three people pressed the switch marked four hundred and fifty volts.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "The man screaming next door had already gone silent.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So would you hurt someone just because an authority told you to?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1961, Yale psychologist Stanley Milgram recruited 40 men for a memory study.",
   "say": "In nineteen sixty-one, Yale psychologist Stanley Milgram recruited forty men for a memory study.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "Each shocked a learner for wrong answers, 15 volts higher each time.",
   "say": "Each shocked a learner for wrong answers, fifteen volts higher each time.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "The learner was an actor and the shocks were fake, but the volunteers didn't know.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Milgram was testing a question from the Eichmann trial, then under way in Jerusalem.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "But obedience wasn't fixed.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "In a shabby office in Bridgeport instead of Yale, it fell to 48 percent.",
   "say": "In a shabby office in Bridgeport instead of Yale, it fell to forty-eight percent.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "With orders by phone, about 20; with two fellow teachers refusing, just 10.",
   "say": "With orders by phone, about twenty; with two fellow teachers refusing, just ten.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the strongest clue was in the words.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "The scientist's prods ran from please continue, to you have no other choice.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Later analysis found that when told they had no choice, people stopped.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So they weren't simply obeying; they were serving a cause they believed in: science.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But in 2012, a researcher reading Milgram's files found many volunteers doubted the shocks were real.",
   "say": "But in twenty twelve, a researcher reading Milgram's files found many volunteers doubted the shocks were real.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So it shows less blind obedience than how far we'll go for something we trust.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "What cause would get you to press that switch?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
